hello i'm greg sadler i am the  translator editor in a large respect  author  of large portions of this book  reason fulfilled by revelation  which discusses the 1930s philosophy  debates that took place in france  i'm going to talk in this section this  video about  the answer to a question that every  author should have handy i think all the  time  and that is  why should anyone want to read your book  what is so valuable in this this text  that i should go and get it and spend  the time reading through it  and i like to think of this in terms of  three sort of concentric circles  of of people one is very wide the the  general educated literate interested  public  another is a little bit more specialized  um  people in  the the fields broadly speaking of  religion  and then there's scholars and that's a  pretty small group and  as with a lot of book projects the  scholarly book projects this did not  start out as something that i thought  would have  very widespread general  interest  as a matter of fact i started  translating these documents and i'll  tell you the story about that in a  different video i started translating  these as sort of a  labor of love something that i was very  interested in doing myself  and i thought not too many other people  would be particularly interested and i  saw some great  implications for the the research that i  was i was doing  um  but  you know i thought it would be too  technical i thought that it might be a  little bit too abstract for for many  people  i was surprised as i've been talking and  doing presentations and people have been  asking me about the book and about the  the topics of christian philosophy just  how how much interest there is in in  this topic and how accessible  um  quite a lot of the the philosophy and  the discussions are as a matter of fact  one of the discussions that's in here  is a more popular discussion by by a guy  named bruno desolage  who was the director at the catholic  institute in toulouse  he was addressing the the  general public  and french philosophers quite a few of  them had an obligation to do so let me  put that aside though um  so  why would an ordinary  person who  has some interest in the life of the  mind be interested in in this book in  particular  well  um what this book makes available to you  are documents that were previously  untranslated that are part of a very  important debate about the nature of  christian philosophy whether it existed  whether it could possibly be rational so  if you're somebody  who is interested in  religion and modernity or  the relationship between reason and  faith or philosophy and christianity  then actually these debates are very  relevant to you  the issues that that are involved  in the very notion of christian  philosophy have never been examined  by as many people with as much depth and  as great detail  as they were during these debates  so  i think that really  if you're interested in  these great broad currents of ideas this  this is a book that you would like to  have and you would like to read  these uh these great philosophers who  were debating with each other in part  because  we often think of philosophers as being  in this ivory tower going off by  themselves but really the way that they  do their work is when they have  arguments with each other and they have  to  they have to respond to each other and  that's what was taking place during  these debates and that's part of what  hasn't been happening  at quite such a fever pitch  really since the 1930s so  that the book is well worth having just  for that alone so that you can see  what the various positions are that one  could take on these these  matters are  i think if you're somebody who's  historically inclined  my historical introduction and also some  of the discussions of the the  relationship between  philosophy  and history and some of the people that  are are discussed  historical figures as christian  philosophers as examples in in the  translations  you will probably find that very  interesting there's a real story to tell  there i don't tell the full story  uh in part because the book has to be  you know the documents and the  historical introduction is is you know  the historical introduction thematic  introduction are about 100 pages about  50 for the historical about 50 for the  thematic introduction  um and i am going to work on another  book i've already started  preliminary research on that which will  be an actual history of the debates  but  until that comes out if you're  interested in how did these debates take  place what was the historical context  why did french intellectual society  develop to such a point where it was  primed for a debate about christian  philosophy of all things to come to the  fore and be  the  cause celeb for about four years five  years of intellectual history well then  i think you'll want to read this book  um  i think that  now we're starting to to narrow the  circle  if you are somebody who  isn't necessarily a theologian or a  philosopher or a historian somebody  who's not a scholar full-time  uh  involved in teaching but you're somebody  who  is involved in some way with the  intellectual life of  a religious denomination  then you probably also want to know  about  the issue of christian philosophy  this is something that is  is core in  the tradition that i belong to the the  catholic tradition that's so important  actually that  that popes have written encyclicals  about it um  but it's also important in a lot of  protestant traditions it's very  important for  lutherans  for reformed christians  you see the wesleyans actually becoming  very interested in the conception of  christian philosophy in the last  15 years or so articles coming out about  that  i think evangelicals have a strong  interest in in this you know question  not necessarily about philosophy but  about reason rationality  is religion on the one hand and  rationality on the other do those merge  at all do they have confluences  or are they necessarily in conflict  are there models that we can find in  past philosophers whether in antiquity  in the patristic era in the middle ages  in the great you know scholastic or or  monastic  philosophers like anselm aquinas  bonaventure um or in later times uh you  know in the 19th century or in the 20th  century with some of these figures  blondel  marcel  jilson do they offer us models for for  christian philosophy  well i think if you are  a seminarian i think that if you are a  pastor i think that if you are  even somebody who is is involved in some  other kind of ministry  if it's any way involved with with  reason or intellectual life i think this  is a book that you probably would want  to read  i think it will be  fruitful for you uh the other groups  that i i think would be particularly  interested in so now we're narrowing yet  to an even smaller group are our  scholars  and i think that  philosophers theologians and historians  particularly historians of ideas would  be would be interested in this  and i i  in the book i actually talk about four  groups who i think would be particularly  interested one is is catholic  philosophers and theologians because  many of the figures writing in this  that i've translated are in fact  catholic thinkers  quite a few of whom actually are  mentioned in fides erazio  i think that  there  for a long time maurice blondel's voice  and those who are are closely aligned  with him like uh  uh antonin sertian and to a lesser  extent gabriel marcel have kind of  fallen silent  and part of what i tried to do by by  translating this was to restore their  voices in english so that not only would  we have  great works about christian philosophy  like atn joe sones spirit of medieval  philosophy a classic that i recommend to  everybody or jacques moore attends  an essay on christian philosophy but  we'd also have the some of the the works  of other figures who took part part in  those debates like like blown down like  marcel like certian  also we'd know more about the opponents  of christian philosophy i've translated  some of those  so again if you're a catholic  philosopher or theologian i think you'd  probably want to to  purchase this book  if you're a protestant  there are a lot of protestant  discussions now about christian  philosophy particularly among  reformed protestants but also among  evangelicals wesleyans  lutherans anglicans  all different types actually there's  even an essay out about a pentecostal  christian philosophy  why would you want this then well again  i think that  what's been lost is that  these issues  were never debated quite as as fiercely  as they were by these thinkers and it  wasn't just catholic thinkers actually  involved in the 1930s debates there  weren't just catholics  and rationalists as we would call them  now seculars there were actually some  reformed protestant thinkers  involved  jacques bois comes to mind pure garin  comes to mind notice these are all  french names these are not names that we  find  talked about a lot over over here  the other reason why i think  that protestants might find this very  interesting protestant scholars might  find it very interesting is there was  actually another set of debates that  took place roughly from about  1947 on through the 1950s  that were occasioned by  another book  um  by a guy named mel who  was responding to to the catholic  thinkers and the rationalist thinkers  in the debates  he wrote a book which was translated  into english and called the condition of  the christian philosopher  they had started another set of debates  among reformed protestants  french-speaking reformed protestants  including um  paul recur  who later on goes on to become a very  famous phenomenologist and hermeneutic  philosopher  he had his early  writings he was very interested in  christian philosophy  um  so yeah  catholic theologians and philosophers  reformed uh or other protestant uh  theologians and philosophers who also be  interested in this i think if you're  doing continental philosophy  one of the things that often gets left  out in what we call continental  philosophy is the fact that  there were many thinkers  and all these people were talking to  each other all the time constantly they  were interacting with each other  there are many thinkers involved in  these these debates who  qualify as continental philosophers like  like maurice blondell for example  blondel  had connections with mark schaeler he  had connections with heidegger through  anastograssi  there was a lot of interplay between  phenomenologists  and even marxists and catholic  tomists augustinians  maurice blondel of course uh there were  there was some overlap as well with  people like edith stein and gabriel  marcel where you had had christians who  were actually phenomenologists  so i think that if you want to  think of yourself as a well-read  continental philosopher if that's your  specialty  you want to know these debates you want  to know the figures who are involved in  these debates  the last group of scholars who i think  would probably benefit by by having  this book and reading through it  would be  people in the broader field of religious  studies or theology or philosophy of  religion so if you're doing those  sorts of  disciplines if you're involved in that  if that's what your formation is  this is important work i i'm not just  pushing it because i you know it's my my  book  it's actually in large respect not my  book  i wrote i wrote the introduction i  contextualized things historically and  thematically  i translated the documents but why did i  translate these documents  so i could take all of these great  thinkers and their voices and bring them  into  an english  idiom to to allow those who can't read  french and that's most of us  to  have access to this wealth of  thought  this brilliance that took place these  these great contests these great debates  that took place in the 1930s  and i think that if you're doing  philosophy of religion i think if you're  doing religious studies  you want to know this material
 

